\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Time evolution of the spurious velocity field for the stationary hydrostatic balance using the standard SPH formulation, vertical axis is logarithmic. For standard SPH, the maximum and root-mean-square velocities exhibit serious growth, sitting at approximately $\qty {10}{\metre \per \second } - \qty {15}{\metre \per \second }$ at $t < \qty {50}{\second }$ and diverging rapidly at $t \approx \qty {50}{\second }$. For In contrast, the corresponding well-balanced (WB) variant maintains these norms at machine epsilon throughout the simulation.}}{90}{figure.caption.96}\protected@file@percent }
\zref@newlabel{fig:STATIONARY-NORMS}{\default{6.4}\page{90}\title{Time evolution of the spurious velocity field for the stationary hydrostatic balance using the standard SPH formulation, vertical axis is logarithmic. For standard SPH, the maximum and root-mean-square velocities exhibit serious growth, sitting at approximately $\qty {10}{\metre \per \second } - \qty {15}{\metre \per \second }$ at $t < \qty {50}{\second }$ and diverging rapidly at $t \approx \qty {50}{\second }$. For In contrast, the corresponding well-balanced (WB) variant maintains these norms at machine epsilon throughout the simulation}\abspage{98}\zc@counter{figure}\thecounter{6.4}\zc@type{figure}\zc@cntval{4}\zc@pgval{90}\zc@enclval{{6}{0}}\zc@pgfmt{2}\anchor{figure.caption.96}}
